@id     04_arrays
@title  Arrays
@topic  arrays
@lang   c

@theory
An array stores several values of the same type next to each other in memory.

    int a[5];        // indexes 0, 1, 2, 3, 4
    a[0] = 7;

The last valid index is size - 1. Reading a[5] here is a bug: it touches memory
that does not belong to the array. CodeLens builds your code with AddressSanitizer,
so this kind of bug is reported instead of silently giving garbage.

Your task: read n (1..100), then n integers, and print the largest one.

Input:   4
         3 9 -2 5
Output:  9

@starter
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
    int n;
    scanf("%d", &n);

    int a[100];
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        scanf("%d", &a[i]);
    }

    // find the largest element

    return 0;
}

@solution
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
    int n;
    scanf("%d", &n);
    int a[100];
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) scanf("%d", &a[i]);
    int best = a[0];
    for (int i = 1; i < n; i++)
        if (a[i] > best) best = a[i];
    printf("%d\n", best);
    return 0;
}

@test
@input
4
3 9 -2 5
@expected
9

@test
@input
3
-5 -1 -7
@expected
-1

@hidden_test
@input
1
42
@expected
42
